1. Singleton is used when we want to have only one instance of an object, example JDBC connection.
2. Eager initialization is not scalable as too many objects present even when not required.
3. Lazy initialization approach discussed in Shape.java class works, but is not thread safe.
4. Usage of a synchronized block is implemented in the  ShapeThreadSafe.java class.


Ways to break the sealed pattern:

1. We can use the reflection apis:
Allows us to access the members of a class and change their properties.
Can access the constructor and then can change it's scope.
example:
    Constructor<Object> constructor =  Object.class.getDeclaredConstructor();
    constructor.setAccessibility(true);
    constructor.newInstance();

How to save?
If object is there throw an exception from constructor
Or
Use enum

Make the class as enum.





